==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|neoterical|neoterick||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|LL.|neotericus}}, from Hellenistic Greek {{m|grc|νεωτερικός}}, from comparative of {{uder|en|grc|νέος||new}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/niːə(ʊ)ˈtɛɹɪk/|a=UK}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Modern, [[new-fangled]]. {{defdate|1590s<ref name="Etymonline">{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1873|author=Fitzedward Hall|title=Modern English|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Fxo_AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA294&dq=%22our+neoteric+verbs%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjHyPPw6fvlAhUyWN8KHcnvAtIQ6AEwAXoECAgQAg#v=onepage&q=%22our%20neoteric%20verbs%22&f=false|page=294
|passage=Among our '''neoteric''' verbs, those in ''-ize'' are exceedingly numerous.}}
# New; recent. {{defdate|1590s<ref name="Etymonline"/>}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=August 21 1998|journal=The Toronto Star
|passage=Should it all come crashing in on us . . . will there be enough luddites, whose hands remember, to free us from the chains of '''neoteric''' technology?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1997|author=Espen Aarseth|title=Cybertext
|passage=A few words on the two '''neoteric''' terms, ''[[cybertext]]'' and ''[[ergodic]]'', are in order.}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A modern author (especially as opposed to a classical writer).
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd}}, Bk.I, New York, 2001, p.140:
#*: Galen himself writes promiscuously of them both by reason of their affinity; but most of our '''neoterics''' do handle them apart, whom I will follow in this treatise.
# Someone with new or modern ideas.
# {{lb|en|historical}} any poet who belonged to the [[neoterics]], a series of avant-garde Latin poets who wrote in the 1st century BC such as [[Catullus]], {{w|Helvius Cinna}}, {{w|Publius Valerius Cato}}, {{w|Marcus Furius Bibaculus}} and {{w|Quintus Cornificius}}.

